Z||000000||ThatRickGuy||06-20-2000||10:32 AM||||Well, I got my fiero last week, picked it up, gave it an all round tune up, and took it to the shop to get the exhaust and valve covers gaskets fixed. They took her up on a hoist and promptly called me back, the said they were hearing a knock from the bottom end, not a piston, but some sleeve pin err something, I was too agravated to listen closly. I had to authorise $350 just so they could start the tear down work. They said that would be the max to figure out what's doing it. but the actual repair bill could get up to $1200! I really trust these guys, but that was suposed to be the money for my new brakes and master cylinder damn it!!! So, anyone know of a good place to get a GOOD 2.8 for a decent price? ||||reg||8
Z||000001||triker||06-20-2000||10:46 AM||||Man, I wouldn't have let them tear into it.  You could have brought it home, pulled the pan, and figured out what was wrong for nothing.  Now, unless you pay them to put it back together or pay someone to haul it home, it's there for the duration. If it's not to late, I'd stop them while I thought about my options.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Old Lar||06-20-2000||11:40 AM||||Seeing that you are in Virginia, there are no Fiero masters that I know of in that area. <A HREF="http://twinlakesfiero.tripod.com" TARGET=_blank>http://twinlakesfiero.tripod.com</A> <P>is Bob Steigers web page. He has engines on the shelf.  But he's in Illinois.  Bob Parker's Kick Hill Farms is in Lebanon, Conneticut and Ed Parks is in Toney, Alabama.<P>They all are reliable used parts suppiers.||||reg||1
Z||000003||ThatRickGuy||06-20-2000||12:14 PM||||Thanks guys. As for me doing the work myself, unfortunatly my knowledge of inner engines is more in theory then practice. I could get it out and appart given a few nights, but as for figuring out how to fix it or what's wrong? I'd be guessing. I'm actually from WI tho, so I might be able to get something from the guy in illinois. Thanks for the web page!||||reg||1
Z||000004||ThatRickGuy||06-20-2000||01:09 PM||||Oy oy oy! talk about expencive! I talked to Fiero Bob, he just happened to have a good (30k miles) long block that just got pulled sitting arround for 700, so I talked to my mechanic, he said that's much much cheaper then he would be able to get one, but installation would still be another 900 on top of that. gah! there went the break money, ac money, koni shock money, new tail light money, and gas money!!!||||reg||1
Z||000005||1FST2M6||06-20-2000||02:09 PM||||i had my car wraped up onthe dyno well over redline and the car shut off... (have knock sensor saftey kill) i thought i was toast.. started the car back up and cnag/clang/clang... i spun a rod bearing... called NAPA and for $5.90 and 5 hours on my bad the pan was off the car the new bearings were in (after a bit of snading with some emory paper to fix the scratches on the crank.. not that bad really) buttoned it all back up ran it on the dyno and it has more oil pressure now and runs just fine! ||||reg||1
Z||000006||ThatRickGuy||06-20-2000||07:25 PM||||arg, I've been debating this back and forth in my head all day. Safe 'n sound on one hand for $1700, or take a chance for any wheres from $300-1500 and not be positive. But after weighing it out, money wise, and 1 year longetivity goal (in 1 year I get out of the military and start making the real money, so I can aford the 3.4 swap) If I get my local mechanic to rebuild the engine, he said upto $1200, but if the cylinders need reboring or anything, the price will jump, but it comes with a 1 yr 12k mile warrenty. If I get the new (used) long block ($700) plus $900 labor (and $100 shipping) I only get a garuntee on the parts my mechanic does... not crank/piston/head etc garuntee. So, I call up fiero Bob, and my mechanic, and told them to flip flop my plans. So, I'm crossing my fingers here, but hopefully the rebuild will go well, and I'll be able to swing the $1200. I'll keep ya guys posted.<p>[This message has been edited by ThatRickGuy (edited 06-20-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||ThatRickGuy||06-22-2000||06:32 PM||||okay.. Just thought I'd pass an update befor I begin a massive search on the forums for pulling the engine. The mechanic I was going threw doesn't want to do it the cheapest way possible, (ie: find the problem, fix it) because he can't garuntee anything else and the price may wind up "jumping" and all. So, I called a local machine shop (Norpa) and asked them how much for a rebuild. $1400, worst case senerio, including bearings, seals, hot tank, deck leveling, boring over the cylinders, new pistons, balancing, etc. I figure that's gotta be a pretty decent price. Only problem is that doesn't include pulling the engine, because they are only a machine shop, they do not have the ability to actually pull/put the engine. So, gathering up all of my courage, and a friend who has a little more time w/ engine work then I, we are going to attempt the "Outragiously Expensive" and pull the engine ourselves. Luckily, we have at our disposal a complete auto hoby center, with tranny jacks, hoists, complete tool sets and everything. Unfortunatly, due to poor timing, (If I had only made this decision last week!!) it'll be close as for getting the engine out, rebuilt and back in on time. We have scheduled thursday night, friday night, saturday till evning and sunday till evning to do it, I'm hoping it won't take nearly that long tho. Encouragement would be greatly appreciated and pointers would be enlightening. I'll keep ya posted.||||reg||1
Z||000008||ThatRickGuy||06-22-2000||07:48 PM||||Okay, I just got the car back, followed a suggestion and pulled the spark plugs 1 by 1, found that the knocking was severly reduced when I pulled the #1 spark plug which leads me to beleive that one of the wrist pins on the #1 cylinder is pretty jacked up. Oil presure is good, warm at idle dropped to about 35, cruising stayed above 40. So, crank should be good. decision time again, $1400 wcs for the engine overhaul, means reliabilty and added power, but is expencive and I'd have to pull the engine, $??? to get the #1 wrist pins replaced, much less expencive, but no warenty or guaruntee of reliability. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||zeusrekning||06-22-2000||08:41 PM||||buy the used engine and replace it yourself. Good hands on activity with the fiero.||||reg||1
